Anaphylaxis
Acute Urgent Care
Sudden angioedema/urticaria, Stridor/wheeze, Hypotension

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-017
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe allergic reaction, throat swelling, and breathing difficulty requiring immediate life-saving intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who had inadvertently ingested a known allergen (peanuts) and rapidly developed widespread hives, wheezing, and a sensation of the throat closing. This presentation represented an immediate threat to the airway and cardiovascular system. Urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment and administration of intramuscular adrenaline were absolutely mandated, as any delay in waiting for regular consulting hours would guarantee fatal airway occlusion or anaphylactic shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My throat feels like it's swelling shut, my skin is on fire, and I can't catch my breath!
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden, rapidly progressive allergic reaction that commenced approximately 15 minutes after consuming a baked good. The timeline began with intense itching and the eruption of widespread urticaria across the torso and limbs. Within 5 minutes, the patient developed facial and periorbital angioedema. Currently, the patient describes a terrifying sensation of throat closure, accompanied by a harsh, noisy intake of breath (inspiratory stridor) and an expiratory wheeze. Severe gastrointestinal cramping and one episode of vomiting occurred just prior to my arrival. The patient feels extremely lightheaded and reports a sensation of impending doom.
Functional Status: Sitting on the floor, leaning forward, highly distressed and struggling to breathe.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening airway compromise. Epipen was expired and failed to deploy properly.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 | HR: 125 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 28 | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is in acute respiratory distress, flushed, diaphoretic, with visible swelling of the lips, eyelids, and tongue.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals an extensive, raised, erythematous urticarial rash covering the trunk and extremities. Respiratory auscultation detects marked expiratory wheeze and audible inspiratory stridor indicating upper airway edema. Cardiovascular exam reveals significant tachycardia and hypotension, signaling early distributive shock. The patient is fully conscious but extremely anxious. Expected investigations would show an elevated serum mast cell tryptase if measured within 1 to 2 hours of symptom onset.
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten baked good on the counter. Adrenaline auto-injector is on the floor, misfired.
Medication Reconciliation: Known severe peanut allergy. No daily medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anaphylaxis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Asthma Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prominent presence of urticaria, angioedema, and stridor (upper airway involvement), which are not features of a purely lower respiratory asthma exacerbation.

Panic Attack/Hyperventilation - Ruled out/unlikely due to objective signs of objective hypotension, widespread hives, and marked facial swelling, none of which occur in functional panic disorders.

Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of resting tachycardia rather than bradycardia, accompanied by severe cutaneous and respiratory signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient perfectly meets the diagnostic criteria for anaphylaxis: sudden onset of rapidly progressive urticaria, angioedema, inspiratory stridor, expiratory wheeze, and hypotension following likely allergen exposure. The failure of the patient's auto-injector meant the patient was in active, unmitigated anaphylactic shock. An urgent after-hours home visit was absolutely life-saving. Without immediate physical assessment and the prompt administration of intramuscular adrenaline by a clinician, the patient would have suffered complete airway obstruction and cardiovascular collapse long before morning clinic access was possible.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Adrenaline 500mcg (0.5mg of 1:1000) Intramuscularly (IM) into the anterolateral thigh immediately. Administered IM Promethazine 25mg and IV Hydrocortisone 100mg. Called 000 for Priority 1 transport. Reassessed at 5 minutes; stridor resolving, BP improved to 110/70.
Positive Behavioral Change: By religiously checking the expiration dates on your Adrenaline auto-injectors every single month and replacing them promptly, you ensure you always have a working, life-saving device ready the exact moment an accidental exposure occurs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport for minimum 4-hour observation (biphasic reaction risk).
DME/Safety (Optional): Prescription provided for two new Adrenaline auto-injectors.
Follow-up: Allergy and Immunology specialist review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. If the patient's breathing deteriorates again or they become faint before paramedics arrive, a second dose of Adrenaline 500mcg IM will be administered 5 minutes after the first dose.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Anaphylaxis is a severe, whole-body allergic reaction that requires hospital observation even after the initial treatment works.
Clinical Course: The adrenaline works instantly to open the airway and raise blood pressure, but the reaction can sometimes return (biphasic reaction) several hours later as the medication wears off.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Stay completely seated on the floor. Do not try to stand or walk, as this can cause a sudden, fatal drop in blood pressure during anaphylaxis. We will wait here together for the ambulance.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I gave you adrenaline directly into your thigh muscle. This is the only medication that works fast enough to reverse the swelling in your throat and tighten your blood vessels to bring your blood pressure back up.
Actionable Steps: Try to take slow, controlled breaths now that your airway is opening up. Moving forward, you must carry two in-date auto-injectors with you everywhere you go, without exception.

